            <ArticleTitle>Analgesia during self-cutting: clinical implications and the association with suicidal ideation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>355-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1440-1819.2008.01805.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The present study examined the association of suicidal ideation in self-cutters with clinical features of self-cutting, using a sample of subjects from juvenile correctional institutions. Multivariate analysis revealed that male self-cutters and analgesia during self-cutting were significantly associated with a history of suicidal ideation. These results suggest that male analgesic self-cutters should be regarded as a high-risk subgroup for suicide.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
